<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout>
    <title>Section 1.8: Transformations of Functions</title>

    <p>
        <alert>MTH 124</alert>
    </p>

    <assemblage>
        <title>Vertical Translation of a Function</title>

        <p>
            The transformation <m>y=g(x)=f(x)+a</m> is a
            <em>vertical translation</em> of the graph of <m>f</m>.
            That is, every point <m>(x,f(x))</m> on the graph of <m>f</m> gets
            shifted vertically to the corresponding point <m>(x,f(x)+a)</m> on
            the graph of <m>g</m>.
        </p>
    </assemblage>

    <p workspace="0.15in">
        That is, <m>f(x)+a</m> shifts <m>f(x)</m> <fillin/>
        (up?/down?/right?/left?) by <fillin/>.
    </p>

    <assemblage>
        <title>Horizontal Translation of a Function</title>

        <p>
            The transformation <m>y=h(x)=f(x-b)</m> is a
            <em>horizontal translation</em> of the graph of <m>f</m>.
            That is, every point <m>(x,f(x))</m> on the graph of <m>f</m> gets
            shifted horizontally to the corresponding point <m>(x+b,f(x))</m> on
            the graph of <m>h</m>.
        </p>
    </assemblage>

    <p workspace="0.15in">
        That is, <m>f(x-b)</m> shifts <m>f(x)</m> <fillin/> by <fillin/>.
        **It's <term>opposite</term> the direction you probably think it moves!
        Ex: <m>f(x-2)</m> moves <term>right</term> by 2!**
    </p>

    <p workspace="0.05in">
        The graph on the left shows the vertical translation of the function
        <m>{y=f(x)=|x|}</m>, it is shifted vertically downward by 3, so it
        becomes the graph <m>{g(x) = f(x) - 3}</m>.
    </p>

    <sidebyside widths="30% 35% 30%">
        <image>
            <shortdescription>
                A piecewise graph f(x) shifted down by 3 under the
                transformation g(x)=f(x)-3.
            </shortdescription>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-1-8-0-a">
                <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
                    <definition>
                        f(x) = abs(x)
                    </definition>

                    <definition>
                        g(x)=f(x)-3
                    </definition>
                    <coordinates bbox="[-4,-4,4,4]">
                        <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no"/>
                        <graph function="f" dash="4 4"/>
                        <graph function="g"/>
                        <point p="(-3,3)" alignment="se" style="diamond"/>
                        <point p="(-3,0)" alignment="se" style="diamond"/>
                        <point p="(2,2)" alignment="se" style="box"/>
                        <point p="(2,-1)" alignment="se" style="box"/>
                        <point p="(0,0)" alignment="se">
                            <m>
                                (0,0)
                            </m>
                        </point>
                        <point p="(0,-3)" alignment="se">
                            <m>
                                (0,-3)
                            </m>
                        </point>
                        <vector tail="(-3,3)" v="(0,-3)" stroke="red" dash="4 6"/>
                        <vector tail="(3,3)" v="(0,-3)" stroke="red" dash="4 6"/>
                        <label anchor="(-2,2)" alignment="ne">
                            <m>
                                f(x)
                            </m>
                        </label>
                        <label anchor="(-2,-1)" alignment="sw">
                            <m>
                                g(x)
                            </m>
                        </label>
                    </coordinates>
                </diagram>
            </prefigure>
        </image>

        <p>
            The graph on the right shows the horizontal translation of a
            function <m>f(x)</m> (given by a piecewise linear equation).
            Since <m>f</m> is shifted horizontally to the right by <m>2</m>, so
            it becomes the graph <m>{h(x) = f(x-2)}</m>.
        </p>

        <image>
            <shortdescription>
                A piecewise graph f(x) shifted right by 2 under the
                transformation h(x)=f(x-2).
            </shortdescription>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-1-8-0-b">
                <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
                    <definition>
                        f(x) = 2*(x+2)+1
                    </definition>

                    <definition>
                        g(x)=1/2*(x+2)+1
                    </definition>

                    <definition>
                        h(x)=f(x-2)
                    </definition>

                    <definition>
                        k(x)=g(x-2)
                    </definition>
                    <coordinates bbox="[-4,-4,4,4]">
                        <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no"/>
                        <graph function="f" dash="4 4" domain="(-5,-2)"/>
                        <graph function="g" dash="4 4" domain="(-2,5)"/>
                        <graph function="h" domain="(-5,0)"/>
                        <graph function="k" domain="(0,5)"/>
                        <point p="(-2,1)" alignment="n">
                            <m>
                                (-2,1)
                            </m>
                        </point>
                        <point p="(0,1)" alignment="se">
                            <m>
                                (0,1)
                            </m>
                        </point>
                        <point p="(-3.5,-2)" alignment="se" style="diamond"/>
                        <point p="(-1.5,-2)" style="diamond"/>
                        <point p="(0,2)" style="box"/>
                        <point p="(2,2)" style="box"/>
                        <vector tail="(-3,-1)" v="(2,0)" stroke="red" dash="4 6"/>
                        <vector tail="(2,3)" v="(2,0)" stroke="red" dash="4 6"/>
                        <label anchor="(0,2.5)" alignment="ne">
                            <m>
                                f(x)
                            </m>
                        </label>
                        <label anchor="(0,-2)" alignment="sw">
                            <m>
                                h(x)
                            </m>
                        </label>
                    </coordinates>
                </diagram>
            </prefigure>
        </image>
    </sidebyside>

    <p>
        Can plot the original, pick an individual point, transform it, pick
        another, ....
        until you see the pattern.
    </p>

    <activity>
        <title>Activity 1.8.2. Vertical and Horizontal Translations</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                Consider the functions <m>r</m> (on left) and <m>s</m> (on
                right) given in the figure below.
            </p>

            <sidebyside widths="35% 35%">
                <image>
                    <shortdescription>
                        A graph r(x) that you will transform in the following
                        problems.
                    </shortdescription>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-1-8-2-a">
                        <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
                            <coordinates bbox="[-4,-4,4,4]">
                                <grid-axes decorations="yes"/>
                                <arc center="(0,-1)" radius="2" range="(0,180)"/>
                                <arc center="(0,-1)" radius="2" range="(0,180)" arrows="0" stroke="blue"/>
                                <line endpoints="((-2,-1),(-4,1))" stroke="blue"/>
                                <line endpoints="((2,-1),(4,1))" stroke="blue"/>
                                <point p="(-2,-1)" alignment="s">
                                    <m>
                                        (-2,-1)
                                    </m>
                                </point>
                                <point p="(2,-1)"/>
                                <label anchor="(3,0)" alignment="se">
                                    <m>
                                        r
                                    </m>
                                </label>
                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>
                </image>

                <image>
                    <shortdescription>
                        A graph s(x) that you will transform in the following
                        problems
                    </shortdescription>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-1-8-2-b">
                        <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
                            <coordinates bbox="[-4,-4,4,4]">
                                <grid-axes decorations="yes"/>
                                <line endpoints="((-2,-3),(-4,3))" stroke="blue"/>
                                <line endpoints="((-2,-3),(4,0))" stroke="blue"/>
                                <point p="(-2,-3)" alignment="s">
                                    <m>
                                        (-2,-3)
                                    </m>
                                </point>
                                <label anchor="(-4,3)" alignment="e">
                                    <m>
                                        s
                                    </m>
                                </label>
                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>
                </image>
            </sidebyside>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    On the same axes as the plot of <m>y=r(x)</m>, sketch the
                    following graphs: <m>{y=g(x)=r(x)+2}</m>,
                    <m>{y=h(x)=r(x+1)}</m>, and <m>{y=f(x)=r(x+1)+2}</m>.
                    Be sure to label the point on each of <m>g</m>, <m>h</m>,
                    and <m>f</m> that corresponds to <m>(-2,-1)</m> on the
                    original graph of <m>r</m>.
                    In addition, write briefly to explain the overall
                    transformations that have resulted in <m>g</m>, <m>h</m>,
                    and <m>f</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-8-soln">
                <image width="35%">
                    <shortdescription>
                        A graph r(x) with transformation as described in the problems.
                    </shortdescription>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-1-8-2-a-soln">
                        <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
                            <coordinates bbox="[-4,-4,4,4]">
                                <grid-axes decorations="yes"/>
                                <arc center="(0,-1)" radius="2" range="(0,180)" arrows="0" stroke="blue"/>
                                <line endpoints="((-2,-1),(-4,1))" stroke="blue"/>
                                <line endpoints="((2,-1),(4,1))" stroke="blue"/>
                                <point p="(-2,-1)" alignment="s">
                                    <m>
                                        (-2,-1)
                                    </m>
                                </point>
                                <point p="(2,-1)"/>
                                <label anchor="(3,0)" alignment="se">
                                    <m>
                                        r
                                    </m>
                                </label>
                                <arc center="(0,1)" radius="2" range="(0,180)" arrows="0" stroke="red" dash="4 4"/>
                                <line endpoints="((-2,1),(-4,3))" stroke="red" dash="4 4"/>
                                <line endpoints="((2,1),(4,3))" stroke="red" dash="4 4"/>
                                <point p="(-2,1)" alignment="s"></point>
                                <point p="(2,1)"/>
                                <label anchor="(3,2.3)" alignment="se">
                                    <m>
                                        g
                                    </m>
                                </label>
                                <arc center="(-1,-1)" radius="2" range="(0,180)" arrows="0" stroke="green" dash="4 2"/>
                                <line endpoints="((-3,-1),(-5,1))" stroke="green" dash="4 2"/>
                                <line endpoints="((1,-1),(5,3))" stroke="green" dash="4 2"/>
                                <point p="(-3,-1)" alignment="s"></point>
                                <point p="(1,-1)"/>
                                <label anchor="(3,1.2)" alignment="se">
                                    <m>
                                        h
                                    </m>
                                </label>
                                <arc center="(-1,1)" radius="2" range="(0,180)" arrows="0" stroke="black" dash="8 2"/>
                                <line endpoints="((-3,1),(-5,3))" stroke="black" dash="8 2"/>
                                <line endpoints="((1,1),(5,5))" stroke="black" dash="8 2"/>
                                <point p="(-3,1)" alignment="s"></point>
                                <point p="(1,1)"/>
                                <label anchor="(3,3.4)" alignment="se">
                                    <m>
                                        f
                                    </m>
                                </label>
                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>
                </image>
                <p>
                    <m>g(x)</m> shifts up 2, so <m>(-2,-1)</m> corresponds to <m>(-2,1)</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <m>h(x)</m> shifts left 1, so <m>(-2,-1)</m> corresponds to <m>(-3,-1)</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <m>f(x)</m> shifts up 2 and left 1, so <m>(-2,-1)</m> corresponds to <m>(-3,1)</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    On the same axes as the plot of <m>y=s(x)</m>, sketch the
                    following graphs: <m>{y=k(x)=s(x)-1}</m>,
                    <m>{y=j(x) = s(x-2)}</m>, and <m>{y=m(x)=s(x-2)-1}</m>.
                    Be sure to label the point on each of <m>k</m>, <m>j</m>,
                    and <m>m</m> that corresponds to <m>(-2,-3)</m> on the
                    original graph of <m>s</m>.
                    In addition, write briefly to explain the overall
                    transformations that have resulted in <m>k</m>, <m>j</m>,
                    and <m>m</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-8-soln">
                <image width="35%">
                    <shortdescription>
                        A graph s(x) that is transformed three times.
                    </shortdescription>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-1-8-2-b-soln">
                        <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
                            <coordinates bbox="[-4,-4,4,4]">
                                <grid-axes decorations="yes"/>
                                <line endpoints="((-2,-3),(-4,3))" stroke="blue"/>
                                <line endpoints="((-2,-3),(4,0))" stroke="blue"/>
                                <point p="(-2,-3)" alignment="s">
                                    <m>
                                        (-2,-3)
                                    </m>
                                </point>
                                <label anchor="(-4,3)" alignment="e">
                                    <m>
                                        s
                                    </m>
                                </label>
                                <line endpoints="((-2,-4),(-4,2))" stroke="red" dash="4 4"/>
                                <line endpoints="((-2,-4),(4,-1))" stroke="red" dash="4 4"/>
                                <point p="(-2,-4)" alignment="s"></point>
                                <label anchor="(-4,.3)" alignment="e">
                                    <m>
                                        k
                                    </m>
                                </label>
                                <line endpoints="((0,-3),(-3,6))" stroke="green" dash="4 2"/>
                                <line endpoints="((0,-3),(6,0))" stroke="green" dash="4 2"/>
                                <point p="(0,-3)" alignment="s"></point>
                                <label anchor="(-2,3)" alignment="e">
                                    <m>
                                        j
                                    </m>
                                </label>
                                <line endpoints="((0,-4),(-3,5))" stroke="black" dash="8 2"/>
                                <line endpoints="((0,-4),(6,-1))" stroke="black" dash="8 2"/>
                                <point p="(0,-4)" alignment="s"></point>
                                <label anchor="(-2.3,3)" alignment="w">
                                    <m>
                                        m
                                    </m>
                                </label>
                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>
                </image>
                <p>
                    <m>k(x)</m> shifts down 1, so <m>(-2,-3)</m> corresponds to <m>(-2,-4)</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <m>j(x)</m> shifts right 2, so <m>(-2,-3)</m> corresponds to <m>(0,-3)</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <m>k(x)</m> shifts down 1 and right 2, so <m>(-2,-3)</m> corresponds to <m>(0,-4)</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Now consider the function <m>q(x)=x^2</m> (not plotted).
                    Determine a formula for the function that is given by
                    <m>p(x) = q(x+3)-4</m>.
                    How is <m>p</m> a transformation of <m>q</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-8-soln">
                <p>
                    <m>p</m> shifts <m>q</m> down by 4 and left by 3.  We plug in 
                    <m>x+3</m> into <m>q(x)=x^2</m>, so <m>q(x+3)=(x+3)^2</m>, and then we have
                    <md>
                        p(x)=q(x+3)-4 = (x+3)^2-4.
                    </md>
                    
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </activity>

    <assemblage>
        <title>Vertical Scaling of a Function</title>

        <p>
            The transformation <m>y=v(x)=cf(x)</m> is a
            <em>vertical stretch</em> of the graph of <m>f</m>.
            Every point <m>(x,f(x))</m> on the graph of <m>f</m> gets stretched
            vertically to the corresponding point <m>(x,cf(x))</m> on the graph
            of <m>v</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
            If <m>0&lt;c&lt;1</m>, the graph of <m>v</m> is a compression of
            <m>f</m> toward the <m>x</m>-axis.
            If <m>c&gt;1</m>, the graph of <m>v</m> is a stretch of <m>f</m>
            away from the <m>x</m>-axis.
        </p>

        <p>
            If <m>c&lt;0</m>, the transformed function <m>y=v(x)=cf(x)</m> is a
            reflection of the graph of <m>f</m> across the <m>x</m>-axis
            followed by a vertical stretch by a factor of <m>|c|</m>.
        </p>
    </assemblage>

    <activity>
        <title>Activity 1.8.3. Vertical Stretch</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                Consider the functions <m>r</m> (on left) and <m>s</m> (on
                right) given in the figure below.
            </p>

            <sidebyside widths="35% 35%">
                <image>
                    <shortdescription>
                        A graph r(x) that you will transform in the following
                        problems.
                    </shortdescription>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-1-8-3-a">
                        <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
                            <coordinates bbox="[-4,-4,4,4]">
                                <grid-axes decorations="yes"/>
                                <arc center="(0,-1)" radius="2" range="(0,180)"/>
                                <arc center="(0,-1)" radius="2" range="(0,180)" arrows="0" stroke="blue"/>
                                <line endpoints="((-2,-1),(-4,1))" stroke="blue"/>
                                <line endpoints="((2,-1),(4,1))" stroke="blue"/>
                                <point p="(-2,-1)" alignment="s">
                                    <m>
                                        (-2,-1)
                                    </m>
                                </point>
                                <point p="(2,-1)"/>
                                <label anchor="(3,0)" alignment="se">
                                    <m>
                                        r
                                    </m>
                                </label>
                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>
                </image>

                <image>
                    <shortdescription>
                        A graph s(x) that you will transform in the following
                        problems
                    </shortdescription>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-1-8-3-b">
                        <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
                            <coordinates bbox="[-4,-4,4,4]">
                                <grid-axes decorations="yes"/>
                                <line endpoints="((-2,-3),(-4,3))" stroke="blue"/>
                                <line endpoints="((-2,-3),(4,0))" stroke="blue"/>
                                <point p="(-2,-3)" alignment="s">
                                    <m>
                                        (-2,-3)
                                    </m>
                                </point>
                                <label anchor="(-4,3)" alignment="e">
                                    <m>
                                        s
                                    </m>
                                </label>
                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>
                </image>
            </sidebyside>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    On the same axes as the plot of <m>y=r(x)</m>, sketch the
                    following graphs: <m>{y=g(x)=3r(x)}</m>, and
                    <m>{y=h(x)=\frac13r(x)}</m>.
                    Be sure to label the point on <m>g</m> and <m>h</m> that
                    corresponds to <m>(-2,-1)</m> on the original graph of
                    <m>r</m>.
                    In addition, write briefly to explain the overall
                    transformations that have resulted in <m>g</m> and <m>h</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-8-soln">
                <image width="35%">
                    <shortdescription>
                        A graph r(x) transformed 2 times.
                    </shortdescription>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-1-8-3-a-soln">
                        <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
                          <definition>f(x)=3*(sqrt(4-x^2)-1)</definition>
                          <definition>g(x)=1/2*(sqrt(4-x^2)-1)</definition>
                            <coordinates bbox="[-4,-4,4,4]">
                                <grid-axes decorations="yes"/>
                                <arc center="(0,-1)" radius="2" range="(0,180)" arrows="0" stroke="blue"/>
                                <line endpoints="((-2,-1),(-4,1))" stroke="blue"/>
                                <line endpoints="((2,-1),(4,1))" stroke="blue"/>
                                <point p="(-2,-1)" alignment="s">
                                    <m>
                                        (-2,-1)
                                    </m>
                                </point>
                                <point p="(2,-1)"/>
                                <label anchor="(3,0)" alignment="se">
                                    <m>
                                        r
                                    </m>
                                </label>
                                <graph function="f" stroke="red" dash="4 4"/>
                                <line endpoints="((-2,-3),(-4,3))" stroke="red" dash="4 4"/>
                                <line endpoints="((2,-3),(4,3))" stroke="red" dash="4 4"/>
                                <point p="(-2,-3)" alignment="s">
                                </point>
                                <point p="(2,-3)"/>
                                <label anchor="(3,2)" alignment="se" >
                                    <m>
                                        g
                                    </m>
                                </label>
                                <graph function="g" stroke="black" dash="4 2"/>
                                <line endpoints="((-2,-1/3),(-4,1/3))" stroke="black" dash="4 2"/>
                                <line endpoints="((2,-1/3),(4,1/3))" stroke="black" dash="4 2"/>
                                <point p="(-2,-1/3)" alignment="s">
                                </point>
                                <point p="(2,-1/3)"/>
                                <label anchor="(-4,0)" alignment="se">
                                    <m>
                                        h
                                    </m>
                                </label>
                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>
                </image>
                <p>
                    <m>g</m> is a vertical stretch by a factor of 3, so <m>(-2,-1)</m> goes to <m>(-2,-3)</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <m>h</m> is a vertical "shrink"" by a factor of 1/3, so <m>(-2,-1)</m> goes to <m>(-2,-1/3)</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    On the same axes as the plot of <m>y=s(x)</m>, sketch the
                    following graphs: <m>{y=k(x)=-s(x)}</m>, and
                    <m>{y=j(x) = -\frac12s(x)}</m>.
                    Be sure to label the point on each of <m>k</m> and <m>j</m>
                    that corresponds to <m>(-2,-3)</m> on the original graph of
                    <m>s</m>.
                    In addition, write briefly to explain the overall
                    transformations that have resulted in <m>k</m> and <m>j</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-8-soln">
                <image width="35%">
                    <shortdescription>
                        A graph s(x) transformed 2 times.
                    </shortdescription>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-1-8-3-b-soln">
                        <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
                            <coordinates bbox="[-4,-4,4,4]">
                                <grid-axes decorations="yes"/>
                                <line endpoints="((-2,-3),(-4,3))" stroke="blue"/>
                                <line endpoints="((-2,-3),(4,0))" stroke="blue"/>
                                <point p="(-2,-3)" alignment="s">
                                    <m>
                                        (-2,-3)
                                    </m>
                                </point>
                                <label anchor="(-4,3)" alignment="e">
                                    <m>
                                        s
                                    </m>
                                </label>
                                <line endpoints="((-2,3),(-4,-3))" stroke="red" dash="4 4"/>
                                <line endpoints="((-2,3),(4,0))" stroke="red" dash="4 4"/>
                                <point p="(-2,3)" alignment="s">
                                 
                                </point>
                                <label anchor="(-4,-3)" alignment="e">
                                    <m>
                                        k
                                    </m>
                                </label>
                                <line endpoints="((-2,3/2),(-4,-3/2))" stroke="black" dash="8 2"/>
                                <line endpoints="((-2,3/2),(4,0))" stroke="black" dash="8 2"/>
                                <point p="(-2,3/2)" alignment="s">
                                 
                                </point>
                                <label anchor="(-4,-3/2+0.6)" alignment="ne">
                                    <m>
                                        j
                                    </m>
                                </label>
                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>
                </image>
                <p>
                    <m>k</m> flips the graph over the x-axis.  <m>(-2,-3)</m> goes to <m>(-2,3)</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <m>j</m> flips the graph over the x-axis and shrinks by 1/2.  <m>(-2,-3)</m> goes to <m>(-2,3/2)</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    On the additional copies of the two figures below, sketch
                    the graphs of the following transformations:
                    <m>{y=m(x)=2r(x+1)-1}</m> on the left, and
                    <m>{y=n(x) = \frac12s(x-2)+2}</m> on the right.
                    As above, be sure to label a key point on each graph that
                    corresponds to the labeled point on the original parent
                    function.
                </p>

                <sidebyside widths="35% 35%">
                    <image>
                        <shortdescription>
                            A graph r(x) that you will transform in the
                            following problems.
                        </shortdescription>
                        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-1-8-3-c">
                            <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
                                <coordinates bbox="[-4,-4,4,4]">
                                    <grid-axes decorations="yes"/>
                                    <arc center="(0,-1)" radius="2" range="(0,180)"/>
                                    <arc center="(0,-1)" radius="2" range="(0,180)" arrows="0" stroke="blue"/>
                                    <line endpoints="((-2,-1),(-4,1))" stroke="blue"/>
                                    <line endpoints="((2,-1),(4,1))" stroke="blue"/>
                                    <point p="(-2,-1)" alignment="s">
                                        <m>
                                            (-2,-1)
                                        </m>
                                    </point>
                                    <point p="(2,-1)"/>
                                    <label anchor="(3,0)" alignment="se">
                                        <m>
                                            r
                                        </m>
                                    </label>
                                </coordinates>
                            </diagram>
                        </prefigure>
                    </image>

                    <image>
                        <shortdescription>
                            A graph s(x) that you will transform in the
                            following problems
                        </shortdescription>
                        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-1-8-3-d">
                            <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
                                <coordinates bbox="[-4,-4,4,4]">
                                    <grid-axes decorations="yes"/>
                                    <line endpoints="((-2,-3),(-4,3))" stroke="blue"/>
                                    <line endpoints="((-2,-3),(4,0))" stroke="blue"/>
                                    <point p="(-2,-3)" alignment="s">
                                        <m>
                                            (-2,-3)
                                        </m>
                                    </point>
                                    <label anchor="(-4,3)" alignment="e">
                                        <m>
                                            s
                                        </m>
                                    </label>
                                </coordinates>
                            </diagram>
                        </prefigure>
                    </image>
                </sidebyside>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-8-soln">
                <sidebyside widths="35% 35%">
                    <image >
                        <shortdescription>
                            A graph r(x) transformed.
                        </shortdescription>
                        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-1-8-3-c-soln">
                            <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
                              <definition>f(x)=2*(sqrt(4-(x+1)^2)-1)-1</definition>
                                <coordinates bbox="[-4,-4,4,4]">
                                    <grid-axes decorations="yes"/>
                                    <arc center="(0,-1)" radius="2" range="(0,180)" arrows="0" stroke="blue"/>
                                    <line endpoints="((-2,-1),(-4,1))" stroke="blue"/>
                                    <line endpoints="((2,-1),(4,1))" stroke="blue"/>
                                    <point p="(-2,-1)" alignment="s">
                                        <m>
                                            (-2,-1)
                                        </m>
                                    </point>
                                    <point p="(2,-1)"/>
                                    <label anchor="(3,0)" alignment="se">
                                        <m>
                                            r
                                        </m>
                                    </label>
                                    <graph function="f" stroke="red" dash="4 4"/>
                                    <line endpoints="((-3,-3),(-4,-1))" stroke="red" dash="4 4"/>
                                    <line endpoints="((1,-3),(4,3))" stroke="red" dash="4 4"/>
                                    <point p="(-3,-3)" alignment="s">
                                    </point>
                                    <point p="(1,-3)"/>
                                    <label anchor="(3,3)" alignment="se">
                                        <m>
                                            m
                                        </m>
                                    </label>
                                </coordinates>
                            </diagram>
                        </prefigure>
                    </image>

                    <image>
                        <shortdescription>
                            A graph s(x) transformed.
                        </shortdescription>
                        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-1-8-3-d-soln">
                            <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
                                <coordinates bbox="[-4,-4,4,4]">
                                    <grid-axes decorations="yes"/>
                                    <line endpoints="((-2,-3),(-4,3))" stroke="blue"/>
                                    <line endpoints="((-2,-3),(4,0))" stroke="blue"/>
                                    <point p="(-2,-3)" alignment="s">
                                        <m>
                                            (-2,-3)
                                        </m>
                                    </point>
                                    <label anchor="(-4,3)" alignment="e">
                                        <m>
                                            s
                                        </m>
                                    </label>
                                    <line endpoints="((0,1/2),(-3,5))" stroke="red" dash="4 4"/>
                                    <line endpoints="((0,1/2),(4,1.5))" stroke="red" dash="4 4"/>
                                    <point p="(0,0.5)" alignment="s">
                                    </point>
                                    <label anchor="(-1.8,3)" alignment="w">
                                        <m>
                                            n
                                        </m>
                                    </label>
                                </coordinates>
                            </diagram>
                        </prefigure>
                    </image>

                </sidebyside>
                <p>
                    <m>m</m> shifts left 1, stretches vertically by factor of 2, shifts down 1.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <m>n</m> shifts right 2, cuts in half vertically, shifts up 2.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Describe in words how the function <m>{y=m(x)=2r(x+1)-1}</m>
                    is the result of three elementary transformations of
                    <m>y=r(x)</m>.
                    Does the order in which these transformations occur matter?
                    Why or why not?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-8-soln">
                <p>
                    <m>m</m> shifts left 1, stretches vertically by factor of 2, shifts down 1.  Yes, the order matters a little.
                    When you double the y-coordinate (the vertical stretch), it must be done 
                    before you shift down by 1.  Otherwise you would also be doubling the shift, so you would 
                    be shifting down by 2 instead of 1.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </activity>

    <activity>
        <title>Activity 1.8.4. Combining shifts and stretches</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                Consider the functions <m>f</m> (on the left) and <m>g</m> (on
                the right) given below.
            </p>

            <sidebyside widths="35% 35%">
                <image>
                    <shortdescription>
                        The function f(x) which you will transform in the
                        following problems.
                    </shortdescription>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-1-8-4-a">
                        <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
                            <coordinates bbox="[-4,-4,4,4]">
                                <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="yes"/>
                                <line endpoints="((-2,2),(-4,-2))" stroke="blue"/>
                                <line endpoints="((-2,2),(2,2))" stroke="blue"/>
                                <line endpoints="((4,-2),(2,2))" stroke="blue"/>
                                <point p="(-2,2)" alignment="n">
                                    <m>
                                        (-2,2)
                                    </m>
                                </point>
                                <point p="(2,2)"/>
                                <label anchor="(-4,-3)" alignment="ne">
                                    <m>
                                        f
                                    </m>
                                </label>
                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>
                </image>

                <image>
                    <shortdescription>
                        The function g(x) which you will transform in the
                        following problems.
                    </shortdescription>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-1-8-4-b">
                        <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
                            <definition>
                                f(x)=-1.5*sin(2*pi/2*x)
                            </definition>
                            <coordinates bbox="[-4,-4,4,4]">
                                <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="yes"/>
                                <graph function="f"/>
                                <point p="(1.5,1.5)" alignment="n">
                                    <m>
                                        (1.5,1.5)
                                    </m>
                                </point>
                                <point p="(2.5,-1.5)"/>
                                <label anchor="(-4,-2.5)" alignment="ne">
                                    <m>
                                        g
                                    </m>
                                </label>
                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>
                </image>
            </sidebyside>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Sketch an accurate graph of the transformation
                    <m>{y=p(x)=-\frac12f(x-1)+2}</m>.
                    Write at least one sentence to explain how you developed the
                    graph of <m>p</m>, and identify the point on <m>p</m> that
                    corresponds to the original point <m>(-2,2)</m> on the graph
                    of <m>f</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-8-soln">
                <image width="35%">
                    <shortdescription>
                        The function f(x) transformed.
                    </shortdescription>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-1-8-4-a-soln">
                        <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
                            <coordinates bbox="[-4,-4,4,4]">
                                <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="yes"/>
                                <line endpoints="((-2,2),(-4,-2))" stroke="blue"/>
                                <line endpoints="((-2,2),(2,2))" stroke="blue"/>
                                <line endpoints="((4,-2),(2,2))" stroke="blue"/>
                                <point p="(-2,2)" alignment="n">
                                    <m>
                                        (-2,-2)
                                    </m>
                                </point>
                                <point p="(2,2)"/>
                                <label anchor="(-4,-3)" alignment="ne">
                                    <m>
                                        f
                                    </m>
                                </label>
                                <line endpoints="((-1,1),(-4,4))" stroke="red" dash="4 4"/>
                                <line endpoints="((-1,1),(3,1))" stroke="red" dash="4 4"/>
                                <line endpoints="((3,1),(4,2))" stroke="red" dash="4 4"/>
                                <point p="(-1,1)" alignment="n">
                                </point>
                                <point p="(3,1)"/>
                                <label anchor="(-4,2.8)" alignment="ne">
                                    <m>
                                        p
                                    </m>
                                </label>
                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>
                </image>
                <p>
                    <m>p</m> shifts right 1, multiplies y-coord by <m>-1/2</m>, 
                    (cuts in half vertically and flips over x-axis), then shifts up by 2.
                    <m>(-2,2)</m> goes to <m>(-1,2)</m>, then <m>(-1,-1)</m>, then <m>(-1,1)</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Sketch an accurate graph of the transformation
                    <m>y=q(x)=2g(x+0.5)-0.75</m>.
                    Write at least one sentence to explain how you developed the
                    graph of <m>q</m>, and identify the point on <m>q</m> that
                    corresponds to the original point <m>(1.5,1.5)</m> on the
                    graph of <m>g</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-8-soln">
                 <image width="35%">
                    <shortdescription>
                        The function g(x) transformed.
                    </shortdescription>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-1-8-4-b-soln">
                        <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
                            <definition>
                                f(x)=-1.5*sin(2*pi/2*x)
                            </definition>
                            <definition>g(x)=2*(-1.5*sin(2*pi/2*(x+0.5)))-0.75</definition>
                            <coordinates bbox="[-4,-4,4,4]">
                                <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="yes"/>
                                <graph function="f"/>
                                <point p="(1.5,1.5)" alignment="n">
                                    <m>
                                        (1.5,1.5)
                                    </m>
                                </point>
                                <point p="(2.5,-1.5)"/>
                                <label anchor="(-4,-2.5)" alignment="ne">
                                    <m>
                                        g
                                    </m>
                                </label>
                                <graph function="g" stroke="red" dash="4 4"/>
                                <point p="(1,2.25)" alignment="n">
                                </point>
                                <point p="(2,-3.75)"/>
                                <label anchor="(-4,-4.0)" alignment="ne">
                                    <m>
                                        q
                                    </m>
                                </label>
                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>
                </image>
                <p>
                    Shifts left by 0.5, doubles vertically, then shifts down by 0.75.
                    So <m>(1.5,1.5)</m> goes to <m>(1,1.5)</m> then <m>(1,3)</m> then <m>(1,2.25)</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Is the function <m>y=r(x)=\frac12(-f(x-1)-4)</m> the same
                    function as <m>p</m> or different?
                    Why?
                    Explain in two different ways: discuss the algebraic
                    similarities and differences between <m>p</m> and <m>r</m>,
                    and also discuss how each is a transformation of <m>f</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-8-soln">
                <p>
                    <m>r(x) = -\frac12 f(x-1)-2</m>.  It's almost the same, 
                    but <m>p(x)=-\frac12 f(x-1)+2</m> instead of -2.  They both
                    shift <m>f</m> right 1, flip the y-coord and cut it in half, then shift, but one shifts up 2 the other down 2.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find a formula for a function <m>y=s(x)</m> (in terms of
                    <m>g</m> ) that represents this transformation of <m>g</m>:
                    a horizontal shift of <m>1.25</m> units left, followed by a
                    reflection across the <m>x</m>-axis and a vertical stretch
                    by a factor of <m>2.5</m> units, followed by a vertical
                    shift of <m>1.75</m> units.
                    Sketch an accurate, labeled graph of <m>s</m> on the
                    following axes along with the given parent function
                    <m>g</m>.
                </p>

                <image width="30%" margins="5% 65%">
                    <shortdescription>
                        The graph f(x) for you to transform.
                    </shortdescription>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-1-8-4-c">
                        <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
                            <definition>
                                f(x)=-1.5*sin(2*pi/2*x)
                            </definition>
                            <coordinates bbox="[-4,-4,4,4]">
                                <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="yes"/>
                                <graph function="f"/>
                                <point p="(1.5,1.5)" alignment="n">
                                    <m>
                                        (1.5,1.5)
                                    </m>
                                </point>
                                <point p="(2.5,-1.5)"/>
                                <label anchor="(-4,-2.5)" alignment="ne">
                                    <m>
                                        g
                                    </m>
                                </label>
                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>
                </image>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-8-soln">
                <image width="35%" >
                    <shortdescription>
                        The graph f(x) transformed.
                    </shortdescription>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-1-8-4-c-soln">
                        <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
                            <definition>
                                f(x)=-1.5*sin(2*pi/2*x)
                            </definition>
                            <definition>g(x) = -2.5*(-1.5*sin(2*pi/2*(x+1.25)))+1.75</definition>
                            <coordinates bbox="[-4,-4,4,4]">
                                <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="yes"/>
                                <graph function="f"/>
                                <point p="(1.5,1.5)" alignment="n">
                                
                                </point>
                                <point p="(2.5,-1.5)"/>
                                <label anchor="(-4,-2.5)" alignment="ne">
                                    <m>
                                        g
                                    </m>
                                </label>
                                <graph function="g" stroke="red" dash="4 4"/>
                               
                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>
                </image>
                <p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Shift left by 1.25, so <m>s(x+1.25)</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Reflect and stretch by 2.5, so <m>-2.5s(x+1.25)</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Shift up by 1.75, so <m>-2.5s(x+1.25)+1.75</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </activity>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Combining Functions (Section 1.9)</title>

    </paragraphs>

    <p>
        Note we can also combine two functions by adding, subtracting,
        multiplying, or even dividing functions, and this will create a new
        function.
        For instance if <m>f(x) = 3x + 1</m> and <m>g(x) = 2x - 4</m>, then we
        can take a new function <m>h(x)</m> to be the sum of <m>f</m> and
        <m>g</m>, so
        <md>
            h(x) = f(x) + g(x) = (3x + 1) + (2x - 4) = 5x - 3.
        </md>
        We can also subtract, multiply, and divide.
        For example we may choose to make a new function
        <m>k(x) = \frac{f(x)}{g(x)}</m>, which we could simplify in this case to
        <md>
            k(x) = \frac{f(x)}{g(x)} = \frac{3x+1}{2x-4}.
        </md>
    </p>

    <activity>
        <title>Activity 1.9.1</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                Consider the functions <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> defined by the
                table below and the piecewise linear functions <m>p</m> and
                <m>q</m> defined by the graph below.
                Assume that the lines in the figure pass through whole number
                coordinates where they appear to do so; for example,
                <m>(2,2)</m> lies on the graph of <m>q</m>, and <m>(3,-3)</m>
                lies on the graph of <m>p</m>.
            </p>

            <sidebyside widths="40% 35%">
                <tabular>
                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell><m>x</m></cell>
                        <cell>0</cell>
                        <cell>1</cell>
                        <cell>2</cell>
                        <cell>3</cell>
                        <cell>4</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell><m>f(x)</m></cell>
                        <cell>5</cell>
                        <cell>10</cell>
                        <cell>15</cell>
                        <cell>20</cell>
                        <cell>25</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell><m>g(x)</m></cell>
                        <cell>9</cell>
                        <cell>5</cell>
                        <cell>3</cell>
                        <cell>2</cell>
                        <cell>3</cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>

                <image>
                    <shortdescription>
                        The graphs of p and q for you to combine.
                    </shortdescription>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-1-8-5">
                        <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
                            <coordinates bbox="[-4,-4,4,4]">
                                <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="yes"/>
                                <line endpoints="((-4,4),(-3,1))" stroke="blue"/>
                                <line endpoints="((2,-1),(-3,1))" stroke="blue"/>
                                <line endpoints="((2,-1),(3.5,-4))" stroke="blue"/>
                                <label anchor="(-4,2.)" alignment="e">
                                    <m>
                                        p
                                    </m>
                                </label>
                                <line endpoints="((-4,-4),(-2,4))" stroke="red" dash="4 4"/>
                                <line endpoints="((4,1),(-2,4))" stroke="red" dash="4 4"/>
                                <label anchor="(-3,-2.)" alignment="s">
                                    <m>
                                        q
                                    </m>
                                </label>
                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>
                </image>
            </sidebyside>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="0.2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let <m>h(x) = f(x)+g(x)</m>.
                    Determine <m>h(3)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-8-soln">
                <p>
                    <m>h(3)=f(3)+g(3)=20+2=22</m>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let <m>r(x) = p(x) -q(x)</m>.
                    Determine <m>r(-1)</m> exactly.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-8-soln">
                <p>
                    <m>r(-1) = p(-1)-q(-1)=0.25-3.5=3.25</m>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Are there any values of <m>x</m> for which <m>r(x) = 0</m>?
                    If not, explain why; if so, determine all such values, with
                    justification.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-8-soln">
                <p>
                    If <m>r(x)=0</m>, then <m>p(x)-q(x)=0</m>, or in other words 
                    <m>p(x)=q(x)</m>.  This only happens at <m>x=-2.75</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let <m>k(x) = f(x) \cdot g(x)</m>.
                    Determine <m>k(0)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-8-soln">
                <p>
                    <m>k(0)=f(0)\cdot g(0) = (5)(9) = 45</m>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let <m>s(x) = \frac{p(x)}{q(x)}</m>.
                    Determine <m>s(1)</m> exactly.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-8-soln">
                <p>
                    <m>s(1) = \frac{p(1)}{q(1)}=\frac{-0.5}{2.5} = -\frac15</m>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Are there any values of <m>x</m> in the interval
                    <m>-4\le x \le 4</m> for which <m>s(x)</m> is not defined?
                    If not, explain why; if so, determine all such values, with
                    justification.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-8-soln">
                <p>
                    If <m>s(x)=\frac{p(x)}{q(x)}</m> is not defined, either <m>p</m>
                    is not defined, <m>q</m> is not defined, or <m>q(x)=0</m> because you 
                    cannot divide by 0.  The only possibility here is that <m>q(x)=0</m> 
                    when <m>x=-3</m>, so <m>s(x)</m> is undefined at <m>x=-3</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </activity>
</handout>